   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Highly Aligned, Loosely Coupled: Leading with Context, Not Control\n\n**One-line hook:** When a Netflix employee makes a bad call, the leader's first question is not \"who messed up?\" but \"what context did I fail to give them?\"\n\n## The Story\nBy the last chapter of the third section, Netflix has removed vacation tracking, expense approvals and the need for a boss's sign-off on decisions. One control is left, and it is the most deeply ingrained in management: the habit of the boss **overseeing the work itself**. Checking drafts, reviewing choices, correcting course along the way. Hastings argues that in a company with high talent density and strong candor, even this should mostly go. Instead of controlling people, leaders should give them **context**.\n\nThe difference is easy to describe. A leader who uses **control** tells people what to do, and checks that they do it. A leader who uses **context** explains the goals, the strategy, the priorities and the stakes so clearly that people can work out for themselves what to do, and often find better answers than the leader would have. Control produces compliance. Context produces judgement.\n\nHastings illustrates the mindset with how a leader should react to a mistake. Suppose someone on your team makes a poor decision: signs a bad deal, launches the wrong feature, spends money on the wrong thing. The instinct is to blame the person, or to add a review step so that next time you catch it. At Netflix, Hastings argues, the leader should first ask: **what context did I fail to set?** Did the person understand how important this decision was? Did they know what success looked like? Did they know about the risk I was worried about? Most of the time, he suggests, a talented person who makes a bad call was missing information that the leader had.\n\nTo describe the organisation this produces, Netflix uses the phrase **\"highly aligned, loosely coupled.\"** Highly aligned means that everyone shares the same understanding of the goals and the North Star: what the company is trying to achieve and what matters most. Loosely coupled means that teams do not need to coordinate every move with each other or seek approval from the top. They can act fast and independently because they already know where the company is headed. The opposite, a tightly coupled organisation, needs every decision to pass through central approval, which is safe but slow.\n\nHastings also offers an image. Most companies picture themselves as a **pyramid**, with the CEO at the top making the big decisions and the people at the bottom carrying them out. Netflix sees itself as a **tree**. The CEO is at the **roots**, supporting and feeding the system. The senior leaders are the trunk. The decisions are made out at the **branches**, by the people closest to the work, who know the most about their own area. The leader's job is to keep the tree healthy and aligned, not to decide what each branch does.\n\nThis approach changes how senior leaders spend their time. Instead of approving decisions, they spend much of it **explaining**: why a strategy was chosen, what the company is worried about, which trade-offs matter. Hastings describes his own role partly in these terms, and describes leaders sharing context in meetings and documents so widely that people far down the organisation understand the reasoning behind big choices.\n\nErin Meyer is careful to set limits, and they are some of the most useful lines in the book. Leading with context depends on two conditions. First, **talent density must be high**: if people are not good enough to use context well, the leader has to control more. Second, the business must be one where **innovation matters more than error prevention**. If a mistake could kill someone, as in an airline cockpit, a hospital or a nuclear plant, or if the work is about doing the same thing safely and repeatedly, then **control and process are the right choice**. Netflix's model is designed for a creative company in a fast-changing market, not for every organisation.\n\nThe chapter also explains why the book's loop has to come in order. A leader cannot rely on context if the people receiving it are not excellent, which is why talent density comes first. And a leader cannot improve the context they give if nobody tells them when it was missing, which is why candor comes second. Only when both are strong does \"loosely coupled\" become safe. Leading with context is the end point of the whole system: a company where leaders supply the why, and the people doing the work decide the what and the how.\n\n## Details Worth Remembering\n- Control tells people what to do; context helps them decide for themselves\n- When someone makes a bad call, ask first: what context did I fail to set?\n- \"Highly aligned, loosely coupled\": shared goals, independent action\n- Netflix is a tree, not a pyramid: the CEO at the roots, decisions at the branches\n- Senior leaders spend much of their time explaining strategy and trade-offs\n- Context requires high talent density; otherwise more control is needed\n- Where errors are deadly or safety is the goal, control and process are the right choice\n\n## The Lesson\nWith excellent people, explaining the goals and stakes works better than checking their work, and a bad decision is often a sign of missing context rather than a bad employee. But this only works when talent is high and the work rewards innovation. In safety-critical settings, rules and process still win.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You find yourself reviewing every piece of work your team produces\n- Someone made a poor decision and you're tempted to add an approval step\n- Teams keep waiting on each other or on you before they can move\n- You're deciding how much freedom to give a new or junior team\n- Your work involves safety risks and you're wondering how far to relax control\n\n## Try This\n- After the next mistake on your team, write down what context the person was missing before discussing what they did wrong\n- Write a one-page note explaining your team's goals, priorities and biggest worries, and share it widely\n- Sort your team's work into \"innovation\" and \"error must never happen,\" and decide where context or control fits\n\n## Related\n- [[Leading Up: When Headquarters \"Just Doesn't Get It\"]]: the responsibility to pass context up and down the chain\n- [[Extreme Ownership]]: decentralised command built on clear intent\n- [[The Andon Cord: Any Worker Can Stop the Whole Line]]: front-line freedom inside a tightly controlled process\n- [[The Toyota Way]]: where standardised process is the foundation of improvement\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What does my team keep getting wrong that might really be something I never explained?\"*"
