   "body": "# Fall in Love with the Process, Not the Finish Line\n\n**One-line hook:** The outcome arrives once, late and on its own schedule; the process happens every single day, so that is where your satisfaction has to live.\n\n## The Story\n*Note: this card retells one of the book's core ideas (the one in its subtitle) rather than a single anecdote from it.*\n\nThe full title of Baker's book is *The 1% Rule: Fall in Love with the Process and Achieve Your Wildest Dreams*. The order of that subtitle is deliberate. The dreams come second. First you have to fall in love with the process, because Baker believes that is the only reliable way to get there.\n\nHe starts by looking at how most people set goals. They pick an **outcome**: a target weight, a revenue number, a promotion, a finished manuscript, a race time. They picture the moment of arrival and use that picture to motivate themselves. There is nothing wrong with having a destination. The trouble, Baker argues, is when the destination becomes the **only source of satisfaction**.\n\nOutcomes have three awkward properties. They arrive **late**, often much later than we planned. They arrive **unpredictably**, after plateaus and setbacks we didn't expect. And they are **partly outside our control**: the market shifts, the judges disagree, an injury strikes. If your happiness is tied only to the outcome, you are signing up to feel unsatisfied for almost the entire journey, and to feel crushed whenever luck goes the wrong way. Even when you do arrive, the glow of achievement fades surprisingly fast, and you start looking for the next finish line.\n\nThe **process** is a different kind of thing. It is the daily practice session, the workout, the pages written, the calls made, the small experiment run. It is fully within your control. It happens today, not someday. And it is where you will spend nearly all of your time. If you add up the hours, the moment of reaching the goal is a tiny sliver; the work leading up to it is almost everything. Baker's argument is simple: **if the process is where you live, you had better learn to love it**.\n\nWhat does loving the process look like? Baker describes it as a shift in attention. Instead of constantly checking how far you are from the finish line, you get absorbed in doing today's work well. You notice small improvements in your technique. You take pride in having shown up. You become curious about how to make tomorrow's session slightly better. The 1% Rule and this attitude fit together: the rule tells you what to do each day, and loving the process gives you a reason to keep doing it when results are slow.\n\nHere is the paradox at the centre of the idea. People who are obsessed with the outcome often get worse outcomes. They quit when progress stalls, cut corners to get there faster, or burn out from the pressure. People who love the process **keep showing up long after the outcome-chasers have left**, and because they keep showing up, the compounding works for them. Detaching a little from the result turns out to be one of the best ways to get it.\n\nBaker is careful not to throw goals away. A dream gives direction and meaning, and he wants readers to dream big. His advice is to **hold the goal lightly and the process tightly**. Let the big vision set the course, then put your daily attention on the next small step. Check the destination now and then to make sure you're heading the right way, but don't stare at it so hard that you trip over today.\n\nHe also connects this to **purpose**. It is much easier to love a process when you know why it matters to you. If the only reason you're doing the work is an external reward, the work will always feel like a toll you pay to get somewhere else. If you understand what the work means to you and who you become by doing it, the daily practice becomes part of the reward. That, more than any trick, is what lets people keep going for years.\n\nThe final shift is in how you define a good day. For the outcome-chaser, a good day is one where the numbers moved. For someone in love with the process, **a good day is one where the work was done well**, whatever the numbers did. That definition is available every single day, and Baker's quiet promise is that if you collect enough of those days, the outcomes you once chased will start arriving on their own.\n\n## Details Worth Remembering\n- The subtitle puts \"fall in love with the process\" before \"achieve your wildest dreams\" on purpose.\n- Outcomes arrive late, unpredictably, and partly outside your control.\n- The moment of arrival is a sliver of time; the process is almost all of it.\n- The glow of reaching a goal fades fast, so outcome-only happiness never lasts.\n- Paradox: people who love the process tend to get better outcomes because they don't quit.\n- Hold the goal lightly and the process tightly.\n\n## The Lesson\nSet a direction, then **move your satisfaction from the finish line to the daily work**. The process is the only part you control and the part you'll spend your life in, and loving it is what keeps you going long enough to arrive.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You feel miserable until a big goal is reached and it keeps not arriving\n- You've hit a goal before and felt strangely empty afterwards\n- You're choosing a career path and wondering whether you'd enjoy the daily work, not just the title\n- A team is demoralised because results are slow even though the work is good\n- You keep cutting corners to reach a target faster\n\n## Try This\n- Write your big goal at the top of a page, then underneath it write the daily process that would get you there. Spend the week tracking only the process.\n- After each session, note one small thing you did better than last time.\n- Ask before committing to a goal: \"Would I enjoy the daily work even if the result took twice as long?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[Bill Walsh: Focus on the Standard, Not the Score]]: a coach who won by obsessing over the process\n- [[Start with Why]]: purpose as the fuel for sustained effort\n- [[Ego Is the Enemy]]: doing the work for its own sake rather than for recognition\n- [[The 1% Rule: Mastery Is Built on Boring Days]]: the daily method this attitude sustains\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"If I never got credit for the result, would I still want to do this work tomorrow?\"*"
