   "body": "# \"Copy It\": Why the Soviet Union Could Never Catch Up in Chips\n\n**One-line hook:** The Soviets stole American chips and copied them carefully, and that strategy guaranteed they would always be behind.\n\n## The Story\nDuring the Cold War, the Soviet Union wanted its own semiconductor industry. It built a science city near Moscow and used spies to obtain American chips and designs. Soviet leaders then told their engineers to **copy them**, rather than invent their own.\n\nMiller argues this was a fatal mistake. Chips were improving at the pace of **Moore's Law**, getting dramatically better every couple of years. By the time Soviet engineers had studied and reproduced an American chip, Silicon Valley had moved on to a newer generation. Copying also meant the Soviets never built the deep know-how, supplier networks and culture of experimentation that drove progress in the US. Their industry stayed permanently **a few years behind**, which mattered greatly for computers and military systems.\n\n## The Lesson\nIn a fast-moving field, **copying the leader locks you into second place**. To catch up, you have to build the capability that produces the next version, not just reproduce the current one.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your strategy is \"do what the market leader does, but cheaper\"\n- You're learning a skill by imitating experts without understanding why they do it\n- Your team relies on reverse-engineering instead of developing its own expertise\n- You're in a market where the technology changes every year or two\n\n## Try This\n- When you copy something, also ask \"What process produced this?\" and try to build that process.\n- Spend some fixed share of effort on original experiments, even if most fail.\n\n## Related\n- [[Originals]]: why genuine originality beats imitation over time\n- [[Think Like a Rocket Scientist]]: first-principles thinking instead of copying analogies\n- [[Start-up Nation]]: how a small country built real innovation capacity\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I copying the output of the leaders I admire, or building the engine that produces it?\"*"
