   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Six Ways to Make a Sale Without Earning a Customer\n\n**One-line hook:** Price cuts, promotions, fear, aspiration, peer pressure and novelty all work, Sinek admits, and that is exactly why companies get hooked on them and never build loyalty.\n\n## The Story\nSinek starts this chapter with a simple claim. If you don't know why your customers are your customers, there are only **two ways to influence what they do**: you can **manipulate** them or you can **inspire** them. Most companies, most of the time, manipulate. He doesn't mean anything sinister. He means the ordinary tactics of business, the carrots and sticks that push people to buy now. He then walks through them one at a time.\n\n**Price** is the first and most obvious. Lower it, and people will buy. It works, and it can work for a long time. But Sinek compares heavy discounting to a drug. Once customers expect lower prices, it's very hard to raise them again, and once a company relies on price to drive sales, it has to keep cutting. Margins shrink. Some industries, he notes, become so dependent on discounts and rebates that they can't sell without them, and the business slowly bleeds. Worse, customers who come for price leave for price. They are loyal to the deal, not to you.\n\n**Promotions** come next: two-for-one offers, free gifts, rebates, \"buy now and get this too.\" Sinek explains how companies that use mail-in rebates often count on a large share of buyers never actually sending them in. The industry even has a name for the unclaimed money: **\"breakage.\"** The promotion drives the sale, and the company profits from the fact that people won't follow through. It works, but it trains customers to wait for the next promotion.\n\n**Fear** is the third tool. If you can make people afraid of what will happen if they don't buy, they'll often act. Insurance, home security, health products and political campaigns all lean on it. Fear is powerful because it speaks to deep instincts, but it creates compliance, not commitment. People buy to make the fear go away, not because they believe in you.\n\n**Aspirations** are the opposite of fear: selling the life people wish they had. Lose weight fast. Six steps to a better you. The new year's gym membership. Sinek points out that aspirational messages work best on people who lack discipline, because they promise a result without asking for a real change in habits. So they tend to work once and fade, and people come back for the next quick promise. He contrasts this with those who already have the discipline and simply want help along the way.\n\n**Peer pressure** is the fifth: telling people that most others already use something, that experts recommend it or that a celebrity endorses it. We feel safer doing what others do, so this works. But again, the customer is following the crowd, not choosing you.\n\nThe last tool is **novelty**. Companies add features, change designs or launch something new to stir up excitement. Sinek draws a sharp line between real **innovation**, which changes the way an industry or a society works, and **novelty**, which just adds something new. His example is the **Motorola RAZR**, the ultra-thin flip phone that sold in enormous numbers when it came out. It was a hit, but it was novelty. Motorola had nothing to follow it, and once the excitement faded, the company struggled. He also mentions the race among razor makers to add another blade and another, each a novelty that competitors can match. Novelty gets attention, but it can be copied quickly and it wears off.\n\nHere's the twist that gives the chapter its weight. Sinek **doesn't say manipulations fail**. They work. That's the problem. Because they work, companies keep using them, until they become part of the business model and nobody can imagine selling without them. But each one produces a **transaction, not loyalty**. Every sale has to be bought again, often at a higher cost than last time. The company is always paying to get customers back.\n\nHe defines the alternative carefully. **Loyalty** is when people are willing to turn down a better product or a better price to keep doing business with you. It's not the same as repeat business. A customer who comes back because you're the cheapest or nearest is repeating, not loyal. Loyalty comes only from inspiration: from people believing what you believe. That's the opening for the Golden Circle in the chapters that follow.\n\n## Details Worth Remembering\n- Only two ways to influence behavior: **manipulate or inspire**.\n- The six manipulations: **price, promotions, fear, aspirations, peer pressure, novelty**.\n- \"Breakage\": rebates that customers never claim, which companies count on.\n- Innovation changes an industry; novelty just adds something new.\n- The Motorola RAZR sold hugely and then left Motorola with no follow-up.\n- Loyalty means turning down a better price or product to stay with you; repeat business is not loyalty.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Manipulations buy transactions; only inspiration earns loyalty.** Short-term tactics are tempting because they work, but each one must be paid for again and again. If your business only grows when you push, you're renting customers, not winning them.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your sales spike only when you run a discount or promotion\n- You're planning a campaign built on fear or urgency\n- Your product roadmap is mostly new features to keep up with rivals\n- You confuse repeat customers with loyal ones\n- You're trying to persuade someone and reaching for pressure tactics\n\n## Try This\n- Audit your last three marketing pushes and label each one with the manipulation it used. How many relied on something other than belief?\n- Ask your best long-term customers why they stay. Listen for answers that aren't about price or convenience.\n- Before adding a feature, ask: is this innovation that changes how people live, or novelty a rival can copy next quarter?\n\n## Related\n- [[The Rubber Mallet: Why Japanese Car Doors Didn't Need Hammering]]: manipulations are business mallets\n- [[Predictably Irrational]]: why free offers and decoys move people so easily\n- [[How to Win Friends and Influence People]]: winning people through sincere interest rather than pressure\n- [[Apple Doesn't Sell Computers First. It Sells a Belief.]]: the inspired alternative\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"If I stopped every discount and promotion tomorrow, who would still buy from me, and why?\"*"
