   "title": "The Great Rewiring: What Changed Around 2012",
   "hook": "Haidt shows graph after graph where teen mental health is fairly stable for years and then suddenly bends upward in the early 2010s.",
   "source": "The Anxious Generation — Jonathan Haidt",
   "book": "The Anxious Generation",
   "concepts": [
    "childhood-and-screens",
    "tipping-points",
    "correlation-vs-causation"
   ],
   "situations": [
    "parenting-and-education",
    "reading-data",
    "technology-and-society"
   ],
   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The Great Rewiring: What Changed Around 2012\n\n**One-line hook:** Haidt shows graph after graph where teen mental health is fairly stable for years and then suddenly bends upward in the early 2010s.\n\n## The Story\nHaidt calls the period from roughly **2010 to 2015** the **\"Great Rewiring of Childhood.\"** In those years, teens moved from basic flip phones to **smartphones** with front-facing cameras, constant internet access and social media apps like Instagram. Childhood shifted from being play-based to being phone-based.\n\nHe presents data from the US and several other countries showing that rates of teen **anxiety, depression and self-harm** rose sharply around the early 2010s, especially among girls. He argues the timing points to phones and social media as a major cause, since they arrived at the same moment across many countries. Haidt addresses critics who say this is only correlation, arguing that experiments, the timing and the mechanisms together make a strong case. Other researchers dispute how large the effect is, and Haidt acknowledges the debate while maintaining his conclusion.\n\n## The Lesson\nWhen many trends **turn at the same moment**, look for what changed at that moment. And when data is debated, weigh timing, mechanism and experiments together.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You see a sudden shift in a metric and need to find the cause\n- You're deciding family rules around phones and social media\n- You're debating whether a correlation in your data is causal\n- You're making the case for a policy change at a school or company\n\n## Try This\n- When a chart bends, list everything that changed just before the bend. Test the strongest candidates.\n- Look for the same pattern in different groups or places; if it repeats, the cause is more likely real.\n\n## Related\n- [[The Data Detective]]: reading charts carefully and fairly\n- [[How to Lie with Statistics]]: the traps of correlation and causation\n- [[The Tipping Point]]: how rapid changes spread through a population\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What changed right before the curve bent, and have I tested it properly?\"*"
