   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Vitol in Libya: Selling Fuel to a Rebellion\n\n**One-line hook:** In the middle of a civil war, an oil trader agreed to supply fuel to rebels who had no government, no bank and no guarantee they would win.\n\n## The Story\nIn 2011, as the uprising against Muammar Gaddafi spread across Libya, the rebels based in **Benghazi** faced a problem: they controlled some oil but had no way to sell it and no fuel to keep their fighters and cities running. Blas and Farchy describe how **Vitol**, the world's largest independent oil trader, stepped in. With political backing from Western governments and help from Qatar, Vitol supplied fuel to the rebels and agreed to be repaid in **crude oil** they could export later.\n\nThe deal was a huge gamble. If Gaddafi had won, Vitol might never have been paid. But it worked: the rebels won, and the authors argue the deal showed how traders can become **players in geopolitics**, not just neutral middlemen. They did what governments could not easily do themselves, and earned profit and influence for taking that risk.\n\n## The Lesson\nBeing willing to act **when nobody else will provide a solution** can make you indispensable, but you have to understand and price the risk honestly.\n\n## When to Use This\n- A customer or partner is in crisis and every normal provider has walked away\n- You're deciding whether to back an uncertain project early\n- You're evaluating how private companies shape conflicts and politics\n- You need creative payment terms when cash isn't available\n\n## Try This\n- When someone can't pay in cash, ask what else they have of value (future output, equity, access) and structure the deal around it.\n- Before a big bet, write the \"if we lose\" scenario in one paragraph and decide if you can survive it.\n\n## Related\n- [[Give and Take]]: helping someone at their lowest point builds lasting bonds\n- [[Zero to One]]: valuable bets are the ones others don't believe in\n- [[The Bomber Mafia]]: how private innovation and war interact\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Who needs help right now that no one else will give, and can I afford the risk of giving it?\"*"
